{"purpose":"Define the binding requirements for the agent-agnostic EKA AI Skill Pack adjustment: role-based command delegation with a per-ecosystem mapping layer, plugin-owned frontmatter rendering and installation, and an explicit solo-mode degrade path — so eka-discuss/eka-execute and the eka-* skills work identically across agent ecosystems without hardcoded agent names. Revision 2 amends the pack after the completed execution of ctr:agent-agnostic-skill-pack (2026-08-23): it legalizes optional named-agent delegation for Codex via installer-emitted agent definitions, adds empirical verifications for Claude Code delivery format and Codex TOML agent semantics, and records the migration status of the first wave.","content":"# Requirement: Agent-Agnostic Skill Pack\n\n## 1. Background\n\nApproved research `fnd:agent-agnostic-skill-pack` validated that the EKA AI Skill Pack (skills/ tree + commands eka-discuss.md + eka-execute.md, embedded in the eka-mcp binary) must be adjusted for the MCP era: commands currently hardcode opencode sub-agent names and pin provider frontmatter (`agent: alex`), breaking portability across ecosystems (opencode, Claude Code, Codex, future MCP-driven agents).\n\nLineage correction: **ADR-030** (accepted) supersedes ADR-023 — the `eka install` command was removed (commit 6600f38) and the plugin contract was relocated to the plugin executable; installer semantics therefore live with the pack-distribution vehicle, not in eka-cli.\n\nExecution addendum (revision 2): container `ctr:agent-agnostic-skill-pack` completed 2026-08-23 and shipped the first wave — role-contract bodies (9 closed roles), per-ecosystem mapping tables (`mappings/*.toml`), skills dual-path + load-order protocol, explicit solo-mode degrade, and the target-aware installer with `DELEGATION.txt` sidecars (develop `f923239`). Empirical spike `cmt:sidecar-frontmatter-verification-implementation` REFUTED the single-agent Codex assumption (V4): current Codex releases enable subagent workflows by default (built-ins `default`/`worker`/`explorer`; custom agents via TOML in `.codex/agents/`). This revision incorporates that finding.\n\nIn-force constraints honored:\n- `req:eka-mcp-production` (approved): commands stay agent-layer workflows; MCP exposes primitives only; commands are NOT wrapped as MCP tools; its resource list already includes `eka://skills/\u003cname\u003e`.\n- ADR-022 (accepted): the skill pack defines agent behavior only and adds no runtime code paths of its own; one folder per skill; portability by convention.\n- ADR-030 (accepted): artifact installation is delegated to the plugin executable (`eka-mcp configure` surface); eka-cli exposes NO parallel install surface.\n\n## 2. Binding requirements\n\nR1. **Agent-agnostic bodies.** Command bodies (eka-discuss, eka-execute) contain no hardcoded sub-agent names and no provider-pinned frontmatter beyond `description`. Negative check: canonical bodies must contain zero occurrences of legacy agent names (`alex-*`, `althea-*`). Delegation is expressed exclusively through the role contract. (SHIPPED — verified in wave 1.)\n\nR2. **Role contract.** Exactly 9 roles, each with input / deliverable / escalation definition. The set is closed: adding or removing a role is a breaking pack change (R12). Roles: architect, backend, frontend, security-review, code-review, product-review, qa, devops, documenter. `product-review` absorbs the UX-review duties /eka-execute performs for user-facing work items; analysis-only roles stay analysis-only. Taxonomy stays at 9. (SHIPPED — ratified during wave 1.)\n\nR3. **Canonical frontmatter minimalism.** Canonical bodies carry description-only frontmatter. Provider-specific frontmatter is RENDERED at install time per target and never stored canonically. (SHIPPED — enforced by renderer loud-refusal on non-canonical pack frontmatter.)\n\nR4. **Pre-rendered mapping tables.** The pack ships pre-rendered role→agent tables per ecosystem inside the pack: opencode+maleolabs agents = default reference mapping; claude; codex/default = all-self/solo. Ecosystem adaptation is declarative — no body editing, no drift. (SHIPPED.)\n\nR4a. **Optional named-agent delegation for Codex (amendment, revision 2).** The all-self/solo codex table remains the DEFAULT policy. Additionally, the installer MAY emit Codex agent definitions (`.codex/agents/*.toml`, `name`/`description`/`developer_instructions`) derived from a codex mapping table whose rows resolve to named agents; when those definitions are installed, named role→agent resolution works natively in Codex subagent workflows. Emission must remain declarative (mapping-driven), idempotent, scoped to pack-owned files, and covered by `--dry-run`. Solo remains the fallback for any role that does not resolve — never silent (see R8).\n\nR5. **Installer semantics.** Installation = pure-copy of bodies + render of provider frontmatter + copy of the active mapping table as a NON-.md sidecar (DELEGATION.txt pattern — never a .md file in legacy command dirs, which scan every *.md as a command). (SHIPPED — including reserved-name overwrite semantics documented.)\n\nR6. **Plugin-owned rendering (ADR-030 lineage).** Rendering/installation logic is owned by the pack-distribution vehicle — today the eka-mcp `install`/`configure` surface, co-located with the embedded pack, mappings/, and provider templates. eka-cli exposes NO parallel install surface and gains no top-level `eka install` command. (SHIPPED.)\n\nR7. **Deterministic install UX.** Install runs support `--dry-run` and refuse unsupported targets deterministically, consistent with `sto:mcp-configure-ux`. (SHIPPED — dry-run writes nothing, not even directories; codex+commands refuses before any mutation.)\n\nR8. **Explicit solo mode.** Ecosystems or roles without a resolved sub-agent capability degrade explicitly: the primary agent performs roles inline, recorded as `mode: solo`, never silently. `/eka-discuss` degrades benignly; `/eka-execute` proceeds with primary implementing under worktree isolation rules. (SHIPPED — mode resolution reads the active delegation data: DELEGATION.txt sidecar, else pack mapping table; default solo with stated assumption when no source resolves.)\n\nR9. **Skills dual path.** File installation stays the primary delivery path; skills are additionally reachable as MCP resources (`eka://skills/\u003cname\u003e` serving SKILL.md content with description from frontmatter), consistent with the resource list already mandated by `req:eka-mcp-production`. (SHIPPED — resource listing descriptions now sourced from SKILL.md frontmatter.)\n\nR10. **Skill reference load-order.** Command bodies reference skills via a load-order protocol: installed skill dir first, then MCP resource, else proceed with inline hard rules — commands keep working without skills installed. (SHIPPED — canonical `## Skill loading` section, byte-identical in both bodies.)\n\nR11. **Transport-neutral primitives.** Bodies cite primitives once via a preflight primitive table (primitive ↔ CLI command ↔ MCP tool); no dual inline citation. MCP tool citations are gated to the mcp-production milestone with an explicit reality-check that the cited tool table exists before shipping. (SHIPPED — tables cite only tools verified against `internal/mcp/server.go` handleToolsList.)\n\nR12. **Pack versioning policy.** Role vocabulary changes (add/remove/redefine a role) are BREAKING manifest bumps; adding a new ecosystem mapping is additive, not breaking. One authoritative version source for the pack (today pack.go, kept in sync with manifest.yaml + anvil.yaml). Build-time drift guard shipped as `TestRoleContractMatchesMappingTables` (M3).\n\n## 3. Out of scope (locked)\n\n- Commands as MCP tools (locked by `req:eka-mcp-production`).\n- Commands as MCP resources (open question, revisit later).\n- Per-user mapping override (substitution fallback covers).\n- Automated per-ecosystem test harness.\n- Top-level `eka install` in eka-cli (contradicts ADR-030).\n\n## 4. Deferred to implementation (empirical verifications)\n\n- V1. Claude Code / opencode legacy command-dir scanning of non-.md sidecars — RESOLVED by spike: scanners read *.md only; sidecars safe. (VERIFIED)\n- V2. opencode frontmatter optionality — RESOLVED by spike: `agent:` optional; renderer omits keys that do not resolve. (VERIFIED)\n- V3. Exact sidecar filename/location per ecosystem; overwrite/re-install semantics — RESOLVED by spike + implementation: DELEGATION.txt next-to-commands (opencode, claude), inside skills subtree for codex; idempotent overwrite scoped to pack-owned files. (VERIFIED)\n- V4. Codex single-agent assumption — REFUTED by spike: Codex ships native subagents by default; superseded by R4a. (VERIFIED)\n- V5. Whether B1 manifest registration should surface the install command in `eka --help` (additive, decide later). (OPEN)\n- V6. Claude Code delivery format: commands-dir (`~/.claude/commands/*.md`) vs skills-format (`~/.claude/skills/eka-*/SKILL.md`) as install target, given open discovery bugs (#35346, #14334) in some Claude Code versions. Decide the default claude layout on current evidence before changing it. (OPEN — new in revision 2)\n- V7. Codex TOML agent definition semantics: discovery paths, precedence vs built-ins (`default`/`worker`/`explorer`), update/removal behavior on reinstall — verify against current Codex docs before implementing R4a emission. (OPEN — new in revision 2)\n\n## 5. Migration notes\n\n- M1. Role unification shifts `/eka-execute` product review from `althea-product-specialist` to the single `product-review` mapping row. (DONE — shipped in wave 1.)\n- M2. Packaging cleanup: README mapping-burden section, manifest skill-count sync, stale `eka install` references in manifest/installation docs. (DONE — shipped in `td:packaging-cleanup-sync`.)\n- M3. Mapping-vs-body drift build-time consistency check. (DONE — `TestRoleContractMatchesMappingTables`.)\n- M4. `skills/scripts/smoke-test.sh` section 8 still drives the removed `eka install` surface; port its assertions to `eka-mcp configure --dry-run --json`. (OPEN — new in revision 2; tracked as `td:smoke-test-configure-port`.)\n\n## 6. Priority \u0026 dependencies\n\nPriority: follow-up after mcp-production, riding the release-coordination train (per fnd conclusion). Users without eka-mcp cannot install skills — acceptable today, must be documented. Ownership is defined relative to the pack vehicle: if the pack ever moves (e.g., eka-standard), the renderer moves with it.\n\nRevision 2 follow-ups track under the successor scope authored for the post-wave work (Codex named-agent delegation per R4a, V6/V7 verifications, M4 smoke-test port, installer hardening backlog)."}